OAI_PAPER({"slug": "A-nine-dimensional-counterexample-to-Borsuks-covering-assertion-September-23-2026", "title": "A nine-dimensional counterexample to Borsuk's covering assertion", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/A-nine-dimensional-counterexample-to-Borsuks-covering-assertion-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "156", "family_title": "Borsuk's conjecture fails in dimension nine", "abstract_html": "The compact set of rank-one orthogonal projectors on ℝ<sup>4</sup>, with the Frobenius metric, cannot be covered by ten sets of strictly smaller diameter. It therefore gives a counterexample to Borsuk's conjecture in dimension nine.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The set \\(X\\) is compact, has diameter \\(\\sqrt2\\), and cannot be covered by ten subsets of diameter strictly less than \\(\\sqrt2\\).", "statement": "<p>Flagship result: Theorem 1.1 of the paper.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\operatorname{Sym}_4(\\mathbb R)\\) be the space of real symmetric \\(4\\times4\\) matrices, equipped with the Frobenius inner product\n  \\[\n  \\langle A,B\\rangle_F=\\operatorname{tr}(AB),\n  \\qquad\n  \\|A\\|_F^2=\\operatorname{tr}(A^2).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Its trace-one affine hyperplane is a nine-dimensional Euclidean space.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  X=\\{uu^T:u\\in\\mathbb R^4,\\ \\|u\\|=1\\}\n  \\subseteq\n  \\{A\\in\\operatorname{Sym}_4(\\mathbb R):\\operatorname{tr}A=1\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The set \\(X\\) is compact, has diameter \\(\\sqrt2\\), and cannot be covered by ten subsets of diameter strictly less than \\(\\sqrt2\\). Thus Borsuk’s covering assertion fails in dimension nine.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Proposition 1 with \\(k=4\\). It proves compactness of \\(X=X_4\\), identifies the trace-one affine space with Euclidean \\(\\mathbb R^9\\), and gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{diam}(X)=\\sqrt2.\n\\]</p><p>If ten subsets of strictly smaller diameter covered \\(X\\), the strict-gap reduction in Proposition 1 would supply an admissible map\n\\[\n\\mathbb RP^3\\longrightarrow\\Delta^9.\n\\]\nThis contradicts Proposition 8.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a compact subset of \\(\\mathbb R^d\\) of diameter \\(\\sqrt2\\) that cannot be covered by \\(d+1\\) subsets of strictly smaller diameter.", "statement": "<p>Secondary result: Corollary 7.1 of the paper.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(d\\ge9\\) be an integer.</p><p class=\"li\">All diameters are taken in the usual Euclidean metric.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a compact subset of \\(\\mathbb R^d\\) of diameter \\(\\sqrt2\\) that cannot be covered by \\(d+1\\) subsets of strictly smaller diameter.</p>", "proof": "<p>The case \\(d=9\\) is Theorem 1.</p><p>For \\(d=9+s\\), where \\(s\\ge1\\), translate the projector set of Theorem 1 to\n\\[\nY=\\left\\{P_x-\\frac14I:x\\in\\mathbb RP^3\\right\\}\n\\]\nin the nine-dimensional space of trace-zero symmetric matrices. Every \\(y\\in Y\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\|y\\|_F^2=\\frac34.\n\\]</p><p>In an orthogonal copy of \\(\\mathbb R^s\\), choose vectors \\(v_1,\\ldots,v_s\\) with Gram matrix\n\\[\n(\\langle v_i,v_j\\rangle)_{i,j=1}^s\n=\nI_s+\\frac14\\mathbf1\\mathbf1^T,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf1=(1,\\ldots,1)^T.\n\\]\nSuch vectors exist because this matrix is positive definite. They satisfy\n\\[\n\\|v_i\\|^2=\\frac54,\n\\qquad\n\\|v_i-v_j\\|^2=2\\quad(i\\ne j).\n\\]</p><p>Therefore\n\\[\nZ=(Y\\times\\{0\\})\n\\cup\n\\{(0,v_i):1\\le i\\le s\\}\n\\subseteq\\mathbb R^9\\oplus\\mathbb R^s\n\\]\nis compact and has diameter \\(\\sqrt2\\): each new point is at that distance from every point of \\(Y\\times\\{0\\}\\) and from every other new point.</p><p>A subset of \\(Z\\) of strictly smaller diameter containing a new point can contain neither a second new point nor a point of \\(Y\\times\\{0\\}\\). Thus a cover of \\(Z\\) by at most\n\\[\nd+1=10+s\n\\]\nsuch subsets would leave at most ten subsets to cover \\(Y\\times\\{0\\}\\).</p><p>This contradicts Theorem 1, since translation preserves diameter and the number of covering sets.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The map \\(x\\mapsto P_x\\) identifies \\(\\mathbb P(V)\\) homeomorphically with the compact set \\(X_k\\) in a Euclidean affine space of dimension \\(N_k-1\\).", "statement": "<p>Projector geometry and the strict-gap reduction.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V=\\mathbb R^k\\) have its usual inner product, where \\(k\\ge2\\), and let \\(\\mathbb P(V)\\) denote its space of one-dimensional linear subspaces.</p><p class=\"li\">For a line \\(x=[u]\\), represented by a unit vector \\(u\\), let \\(P_x=uu^T\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Equip \\(\\operatorname{Sym}(V)\\) with the Frobenius inner product, and put\n  \\[\n  N_k=\\dim\\operatorname{Sym}(V)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2,\n  \\qquad\n  X_k=\\{P_x:x\\in\\mathbb P(V)\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For an integer \\(m\\ge1\\), write\n  \\[\n  [m]=\\{1,\\ldots,m\\},\n  \\qquad\n  \\Delta^{m-1}\n  =\n  \\left\\{(t_1,\\ldots,t_m):t_i\\ge0,\\ \\sum_i t_i=1\\right\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Following Definition 2.3 of the paper, a smooth map\n  \\(f=(f_1,\\ldots,f_m):\\mathbb P(V)\\to\\mathbb R^m\\) is admissible if each \\(f_i\\) is nonnegative, \\(\\sum_i f_i=1\\), and, with\n  \\[\n  S(x)=\\{i:f_i(x)&gt;0\\},\n  \\]\n  one has\n  \\[\n  x\\perp y\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad S(x)\\cap S(y)=\\varnothing.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The support complex of an admissible map is the finite simplicial complex\n  \\[\n  I\\in K\n  \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n  I\\subseteq S(x)\\ \\text{for some }x\\in\\mathbb P(V).\n  \\]\n  Thus \\(f\\) takes values in \\(|K|\\subseteq\\Delta^{m-1}\\). No triangulation of \\(\\mathbb P(V)\\) is assumed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The map \\(x\\mapsto P_x\\) identifies \\(\\mathbb P(V)\\) homeomorphically with the compact set \\(X_k\\) in a Euclidean affine space of dimension \\(N_k-1\\). Moreover,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{diam}(X_k)=\\sqrt2,\n\\]\nand two projectors are at this distance exactly when their lines are orthogonal.</p><p>If \\(X_k\\) is covered by at most \\(m\\) subsets, each of diameter strictly less than \\(\\sqrt2\\), then there is an admissible map\n\\[\nf:\\mathbb P(V)\\longrightarrow\\Delta^{m-1}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 1.1 gives the compact projector model, its ambient dimension, and its exact diameter. Lemma 1.2 converts a smaller-diameter cover into an admissible map.</p><p>The strict diameter inequalities are what permit the open neighborhoods and the smooth partition of unity; merely excluding individual orthogonal pairs from arbitrary color classes does not supply this reduction.</p><p>Restriction of an admissible map to \\(\\mathbb P(W)\\), for a nonzero linear subspace \\(W\\subseteq V\\), preserves admissibility. Coordinates that vanish identically on this subspace may be omitted.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1, together with the coordinate description in Remark 2.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The map \\(x\\mapsto P_x\\) identifies \\(\\mathbb P(V)\\) homeomorphically with the compact set \\(X_k\\) in the trace-one affine subspace of \\(\\operatorname{Sym}(V)\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(V\\), \\(k\\), \\(N_k\\), \\(P_x\\), and \\(X_k\\) from Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(u,v\\) be unit representatives of \\(x,y\\in\\mathbb P(V)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The map \\(x\\mapsto P_x\\) identifies \\(\\mathbb P(V)\\) homeomorphically with the compact set \\(X_k\\) in the trace-one affine subspace of \\(\\operatorname{Sym}(V)\\). This affine subspace is Euclidean of dimension \\(N_k-1\\), and\n\\[\n\\|P_x-P_y\\|_F^2=2-2\\langle u,v\\rangle^2. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\nConsequently \\(\\operatorname{diam}(X_k)=\\sqrt2\\), with equality precisely for orthogonal lines.</p><p>For \\(k=4\\), an explicit isometry from the trace-one symmetric matrices to \\(\\mathbb R^9\\) is\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nA\\longmapsto\\bigg(\n&amp;\\frac{a_{11}-a_{22}}{\\sqrt2},\n\\frac{a_{11}+a_{22}-2a_{33}}{\\sqrt6},\n\\frac{a_{11}+a_{22}+a_{33}-3a_{44}}{\\sqrt{12}},\\\\\n&amp;\\sqrt2a_{12},\\sqrt2a_{13},\\sqrt2a_{14},\n\\sqrt2a_{23},\\sqrt2a_{24},\\sqrt2a_{34}\n\\bigg). \\qquad(2.2)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The formula \\(uu^T\\) is unchanged when \\(u\\) is replaced by \\(-u\\), so it gives a continuous map on projective space. Its range as an operator is exactly \\(x\\), which proves injectivity. A continuous injection from the compact space \\(\\mathbb P(V)\\) into a Hausdorff space is a homeomorphism onto its image.</p><p>Every \\(P_x\\) has trace one, and the trace-one hyperplane has codimension one in \\(\\operatorname{Sym}(V)\\). Finally,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(P_x^2)=\\operatorname{tr}(P_y^2)=1,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{tr}(P_xP_y)=\\langle u,v\\rangle^2,\n\\]\nproving (2.1). Orthogonal lines exist because \\(k\\ge2\\).</p><p>In (2.2), the first three coordinates use an orthonormal basis of the trace-zero diagonal matrices. The factors \\(\\sqrt2\\) in the remaining six coordinates account for the two equal off-diagonal entries in the Frobenius norm.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 2.4, the strict-gap reduction", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is an admissible map \\( f:\\mathbb P(V)\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R^m. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the setting of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(X_k\\) is covered by at most \\(m\\) subsets, each of diameter strictly less than \\(\\sqrt2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an admissible map\n\\[\nf:\\mathbb P(V)\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R^m.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Pad the cover by empty sets to write it as \\(A_1,\\ldots,A_m\\). For every nonempty \\(A_i\\), set\n\\[\nd_i=\\operatorname{diam}(A_i),\n\\qquad\n\\varepsilon_i=\\frac{\\sqrt2-d_i}{4}&gt;0,\n\\]\nand define the relatively open set\n\\[\nU_i=\\{P\\in X_k:\\operatorname{dist}_F(P,A_i)&lt;\\varepsilon_i\\}.\n\\]\nSet \\(U_i=\\varnothing\\) if \\(A_i=\\varnothing\\). The sets \\(U_i\\) cover \\(X_k\\).</p><p>For any \\(P,Q\\in U_i\\), points of \\(A_i\\) within \\(\\varepsilon_i\\) of \\(P,Q\\) and the triangle inequality give\n\\[\n\\|P-Q\\|_F\n&lt;\nd_i+2\\varepsilon_i\n=\n\\frac{d_i+\\sqrt2}{2}\n&lt;\n\\sqrt2.\n\\]\nIn particular,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{diam}(U_i)\n\\le\n\\frac{d_i+\\sqrt2}{2}\n&lt;\n\\sqrt2.\n\\]</p><p>Pull these sets back to a finite open cover \\(V_i\\) of \\(\\mathbb P(V)\\). A smooth partition of unity subordinate to this cover gives smooth nonnegative functions \\(f_i\\), with sum one and \\(f_i(x)&gt;0\\) only if \\(x\\in V_i\\). Empty sets receive the zero function.</p><p>If \\(i\\) belonged to both \\(S(x)\\) and \\(S(y)\\) for orthogonal lines \\(x,y\\), then \\(P_x,P_y\\in U_i\\) would be at distance \\(\\sqrt2\\) by Lemma 1.1, a contradiction.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The extension \\(E_V\\) is continuous on the full positive semidefinite cone, nonnegative coordinatewise, homogeneous of degree one for nonnegative scalars, and satisfies …", "statement": "<p>Extension to positive semidefinite and symmetric operators.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V\\) be a Euclidean space of dimension \\(k\\ge2\\), and let\n  \\[\n  f:\\mathbb P(V)\\longrightarrow\\Delta^{m-1}\n  \\]\n  be a smooth admissible map, with \\(S(x)=\\{i:f_i(x)&gt;0\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  H(v)=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  \\|v\\|^2f([v]),&amp;v\\ne0,\\\\\n  0,&amp;v=0.\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a positive semidefinite operator \\(Q\\), define\n  \\[\n  E_V(Q)\n  =\n  k\\int_{S(V)}H(Q^{1/2}u)\\,d\\mu_V(u), \\tag{3.1}\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\mu_V\\) is rotation-invariant probability measure on the unit sphere.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(A\\in\\operatorname{Sym}(V)\\), put\n  \\[\n  |A|=(A^2)^{1/2},\n  \\qquad\n  A_+=\\frac{|A|+A}{2},\n  \\qquad\n  A_-=\\frac{|A|-A}{2},\n  \\]\n  and define\n  \\[\n  F(A)=E_V(A_+)-E_V(A_-). \\tag{3.3}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The extension \\(E_V\\) is continuous on the full positive semidefinite cone, nonnegative coordinatewise, homogeneous of degree one for nonnegative scalars, and satisfies\n\\[\n\\sum_i E_V(Q)_i=\\operatorname{tr}Q,\n\\qquad\nE_V(P_x)=f(x),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\{i:E_V(Q)_i&gt;0\\}\n=\n\\bigcup_{x\\in\\mathbb P(\\operatorname{ran}Q)}S(x).\n\\]\nIt agrees with the corresponding extension on any subspace supporting \\(Q\\), and is smooth when that subspace varies smoothly with fixed dimension and the restricted operator varies smoothly through positive definite operators.</p><p>The map \\(F\\) is continuous, odd, homogeneous of degree one for nonnegative scalars, and smooth on the nonsingular symmetric operators. It satisfies\n\\[\n\\|F(A)\\|_1=\\operatorname{tr}|A|,\n\\qquad\n\\sum_iF(A)_i=\\operatorname{tr}A, \\tag{3.4}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\{i:F(A)_i&gt;0\\}\n&amp;=\n\\bigcup_{x\\in\\mathbb P(\\operatorname{ran}A_+)}S(x),\\\\\n\\{i:F(A)_i&lt;0\\}\n&amp;=\n\\bigcup_{x\\in\\mathbb P(\\operatorname{ran}A_-)}S(x).\n\\end{aligned} \\qquad(3.5)\n\\]\nIf every coordinate of \\(F(A)\\) is nonzero, then \\(A\\) is nonsingular. If all coordinates of \\(F(A)\\) are strictly negative, then \\(A\\) is negative definite; the analogous assertion holds with both signs reversed.</p><p>In particular, \\(F\\) restricts to a continuous odd map\n\\[\n\\{A:\\operatorname{tr}|A|=1\\}\n\\longrightarrow\n\\{y\\in\\mathbb R^m:\\|y\\|_1=1\\}. \\tag{3.6}\n\\]\nRadial normalization identifies these spaces antipodally with spheres of dimensions \\(N_k-1\\) and \\(m-1\\), where \\(N_k=k(k+1)/2\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 2.1 proves all asserted properties of \\(E_V\\), and Lemma 2.2 proves the corresponding properties of \\(F\\).</p><p>The two norm spheres in (3.6) are not regarded as globally smooth hypersurfaces. Radial normalization identifies them antipodally with ordinary Euclidean spheres of dimensions \\(N_k-1\\) and \\(m-1\\). These identifications are homeomorphisms everywhere and smooth diffeomorphisms locally at nonsingular matrices and label vectors with every coordinate nonzero.</p><p>Indeed, \\(\\operatorname{tr}|A|\\) is smooth near a nonsingular \\(A\\), while the \\(\\ell_1\\) norm is a fixed linear form within each open sign orthant. Consequently ordinary sphere degree applies when \\(m=N_k\\), and local differential arguments are valid at every preimage of a full-support value.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.1, properties of the positive extension", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The map \\(E_V\\) is continuous on the full positive semidefinite cone, including its singular boundary.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(V\\), \\(f\\), \\(H\\), and \\(E_V\\) from Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">For a nonzero subspace \\(W\\subseteq V\\), let \\(E_W\\) denote the same construction using \\(f|_{\\mathbb P(W)}\\), rotation-invariant probability measure on \\(S(W)\\), and the factor \\(\\dim W\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The map \\(E_V\\) is continuous on the full positive semidefinite cone, including its singular boundary. For \\(t\\ge0\\),\n\\[\nE_V(tQ)=tE_V(Q),\n\\qquad\nE_V(Q)_i\\ge0,\n\\qquad\n\\sum_iE_V(Q)_i=\\operatorname{tr}Q.\n\\]\nIts positive coordinates are exactly\n\\[\n\\{i:E_V(Q)_i&gt;0\\}\n=\n\\bigcup_{x\\in\\mathbb P(\\operatorname{ran}Q)}S(x), \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nwhere the right side is empty for \\(Q=0\\).</p><p>Moreover:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(E_V(P_x)=f(x)\\) for every line \\(x\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(Q\\) is supported on \\(W\\), then\n   \\[\n   E_V(Q)=E_W(Q|_W).\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. This expression is smooth when \\(W\\) varies smoothly with fixed dimension and \\(Q|_W\\) varies smoothly through positive definite operators.</p>", "proof": "<p>Continuity of \\(H\\) at zero follows from\n\\[\n\\|H(v)\\|_1=\\|v\\|^2.\n\\]\nThe positive square root depends continuously on a positive semidefinite matrix. For example, approximate the square-root function uniformly by polynomials on a compact interval containing the spectra under consideration, and apply those polynomials to the matrices. Thus the integrand in (3.1) is jointly continuous in \\((Q,u)\\); integration over the compact sphere proves continuity of \\(E_V\\).</p><p>Its homogeneity and nonnegativity follow from the corresponding properties of \\(H\\). Since\n\\[\n\\sum_iH(v)_i=\\|v\\|^2,\n\\qquad\n\\int uu^T\\,d\\mu_V(u)=k^{-1}I,\n\\]\nwe have\n\\[\n\\sum_iE_V(Q)_i\n=\nk\\int_{S(V)}\\langle Qu,u\\rangle\\,d\\mu_V(u)\n=\n\\operatorname{tr}Q.\n\\]</p><p>Every nonzero \\(Q^{1/2}u\\) lies in \\(\\operatorname{ran}Q\\). Conversely, every line in that range is spanned by some \\(Q^{1/2}u\\) with \\(\\|u\\|=1\\), because \\(Q^{1/2}\\) is invertible on its range. If \\(f_i\\) is positive on such a line, the \\(i\\)th integrand is positive at that \\(u\\), and hence on a nonempty open set of the sphere. Such a set has positive measure. This proves (3.2) in both directions.</p><p>For \\(Q=P_x\\), every nonzero integrand is a scalar multiple of \\(f(x)\\), and the scalars integrate to \\(\\operatorname{tr}P_x=1\\). Thus \\(E_V(P_x)=f(x)\\).</p><p>To prove the subspace identity, write \\(r=\\dim W\\) and let \\(\\pi_W\\) be orthogonal projection. The finite measure on \\(S(W)\\) defined by\n\\[\nB\\longmapsto\n\\int_{S(V)}\n\\|\\pi_Wu\\|^2\n\\mathbf1_{\\{\\pi_Wu\\ne0,\\ \\pi_Wu/\\|\\pi_Wu\\|\\in B\\}}\n\\,d\\mu_V(u)\n\\]\nis invariant under all orthogonal transformations of \\(W\\). Its total mass is\n\\[\n\\int\\|\\pi_Wu\\|^2\\,d\\mu_V(u)=\\frac rk,\n\\]\nso it equals \\((r/k)\\mu_W\\). Because \\(Q\\) vanishes on \\(W^\\perp\\) and \\(H\\) is homogeneous of degree two,\n\\[\nk\\int_{S(V)}H(Q^{1/2}u)\\,d\\mu_V(u)\n=\nr\\int_{S(W)}H((Q|_W)^{1/2}w)\\,d\\mu_W(w).\n\\]</p><p>Finally choose, locally in the parameters, a smooth orthonormal frame \\(T:\\mathbb R^r\\to W\\). Write \\(Q|_W\\) in that frame as the positive definite matrix \\(C\\). The subspace formula becomes\n\\[\nE_V(Q)\n=\nr\\int_{S^{r-1}}H(TC^{1/2}w)\\,d\\mu(w).\n\\]\nThe matrix square root is smooth on positive definite matrices, and \\(TC^{1/2}w\\) never vanishes. The function \\(H\\) is smooth away from zero. Differentiating this expression over the compact sphere proves the asserted local smoothness.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.2, the odd sphere map", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The map \\(F:\\operatorname{Sym}(V)\\to\\mathbb R^m\\) is continuous, odd, and homogeneous of degree one for nonnegative scalars.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the admissible map \\(f\\), the positive extension \\(E_V\\), and\n  \\[\n  F(A)=E_V(A_+)-E_V(A_-)\n  \\]\n  from Proposition 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The map \\(F:\\operatorname{Sym}(V)\\to\\mathbb R^m\\) is continuous, odd, and homogeneous of degree one for nonnegative scalars. It satisfies (3.4) and the exact spectral-support formulas (3.5).</p><p>If every coordinate of \\(F(A)\\) is nonzero, then \\(A\\) is nonsingular. If every coordinate of \\(F(A)\\) is strictly negative, then \\(A\\) is negative definite, and similarly for positive coordinates.</p><p>The map \\(F\\) is smooth on the open set of nonsingular symmetric operators and restricts to the continuous odd map (3.6).</p>", "proof": "<p>Continuity and homogeneity follow from Lemma 2.1 and the continuous formulas for \\(A_\\pm\\). Replacing \\(A\\) by \\(-A\\) exchanges \\(A_+\\) and \\(A_-\\), so \\(F\\) is odd.</p><p>Any line in \\(\\operatorname{ran}A_+\\) is orthogonal to every line in \\(\\operatorname{ran}A_-\\). Admissibility and (3.2) therefore imply that \\(E_V(A_+)\\) and \\(E_V(A_-)\\) have disjoint positive coordinates. There is no cancellation in their difference. This proves (3.5) and, by summing coordinates, (3.4).</p><p>If \\(\\ker A\\) contains a line \\(x\\), choose \\(i\\in S(x)\\), which is possible because \\(\\sum_i f_i(x)=1\\). The line \\(x\\) is orthogonal to both spectral ranges. Thus label \\(i\\) is absent from both unions in (3.5), so \\(F(A)_i=0\\). This proves the nonsingularity assertion.</p><p>If all coordinates of \\(F(A)\\) are negative, then \\(E_V(A_+)=0\\) by disjointness of the two positive supports. Hence \\(\\operatorname{tr}A_+=0\\), so \\(A_+=0\\), and nonsingularity makes \\(A\\) negative definite. Oddness gives the positive case.</p><p>For local smoothness, fix nonsingular \\(A\\). In a neighborhood of \\(A\\), the positive and negative spectral subspaces have constant dimensions and depend smoothly on the matrix. Their projections are\n\\[\n\\Pi_\\pm(A)\n=\n\\frac12\\bigl(I\\pm A(A^2)^{-1/2}\\bigr),\n\\]\nwhich are smooth there because \\(A^2\\) is positive definite.</p><p>Each projection admits a local smooth orthonormal frame: project a fixed basis for its range and apply Gram–Schmidt after shrinking the neighborhood. In these frames, \\(A_+\\) and \\(A_-\\) restrict to positive definite operators on their respective ranges. The last assertion of Lemma 2.1 proves smoothness of \\(E_V(A_+)\\) and \\(E_V(A_-)\\), with a zero term if the corresponding range has dimension zero.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Proposition 4.1, the number of labels", "depth": 0, "snippet": "One has \\(m\\ge N_k\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\ge2\\), \\(N_k=k(k+1)/2\\), and let\n  \\[\n  f:\\mathbb P(\\mathbb R^k)\\longrightarrow\\Delta^{m-1}\n  \\]\n  be a smooth admissible map.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K\\) be its support complex and \\(F\\) its extension from Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  \\Sigma(V)=\\{A\\in\\operatorname{Sym}(V):\\operatorname{tr}|A|=1\\},\n  \\qquad\n  \\Sigma_m=\\left\\{y\\in\\mathbb R^m:\\sum_i|y_i|=1\\right\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One has \\(m\\ge N_k\\). If \\(m=N_k\\), then\n\\[\nF:\\Sigma(\\mathbb R^k)\\longrightarrow\\Sigma_m\n\\]\nhas mod-two degree one and is surjective. In this equality case, every pair of labels is an edge of \\(K\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>An odd map \\(S^n\\to S^d\\) requires \\(n\\le d\\), and an odd self-map of \\(S^n\\) has odd degree; these are the facts cited in the paper from Hatcher, Proposition 2B.6 and Corollary 2B.7. Applying them to the sphere map of Proposition 2 proves the bound and the degree assertion.</p><p>A map of nonzero degree cannot omit a point of its target sphere, since the complement of a point is contractible.</p><p>Suppose \\(m=N_k\\) and fix distinct labels \\(i,j\\). Choose a vector in \\(\\Sigma_m\\) with every coordinate nonzero, with precisely the \\(i\\)th and \\(j\\)th coordinates positive. At a preimage \\(A\\) under \\(F\\), every line in the positive spectral subspace of \\(A\\) uses only these two labels.</p><p>That subspace cannot have dimension at least two: restriction to a two-dimensional subspace would contradict the bound \\(N_2=3\\) just proved. Its dimension is therefore one. The exact spectral-support property of \\(F\\) says that this line has both labels \\(i,j\\) in its support, so \\(\\{i,j\\}\\) is an edge.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Theorem 4.3, supports at equality", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every pair of labels is an edge of \\(K\\), every facet of \\(K\\) has \\(k\\) vertices, and \\( \\sum_{I\\text{ a facet of }K}I \\) is a simplicial cycle over \\(\\mathbb F_2\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V=\\mathbb R^k\\), where \\(k\\ge3\\), and put\n  \\[\n  m=N_k=\\frac{k(k+1)}2.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(f:\\mathbb P(V)\\to\\Delta^{m-1}\\) be a smooth admissible map and \\(K\\) its support complex.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F:\\Sigma(V)\\to\\Sigma_m\\) be its extension from Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Give the norm spheres the smooth structures transported from ordinary Euclidean spheres by radial projection.</p><p class=\"li\">Throughout this proposition and its lemmas, homology and cohomology have coefficients in \\(\\mathbb F_2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every pair of labels is an edge of \\(K\\), every facet of \\(K\\) has \\(k\\) vertices, and\n\\[\n\\sum_{I\\text{ a facet of }K}I\n\\]\nis a simplicial cycle over \\(\\mathbb F_2\\). Consequently every \\((k-2)\\)-dimensional face lies in a positive even number of facets.</p>", "proof": "<p>The edge assertion is Proposition 3. Lemma 4.3 proves that every facet has \\(k\\) vertices, so \\(K\\) has dimension \\(k-1\\).</p><p>Using the identification of simplicial and singular homology, consider\n\\[\nf_*[\\mathbb P(V)]\\in H_{k-1}(|K|).\n\\]\nFor a facet \\(I\\), choose the regular interior point \\(\\widetilde r\\) supplied by Lemma 4.3. This point has a neighborhood in \\(|K|\\) contained in that facet. Passing to\n\\[\nH_{k-1}(|K|,|K|\\setminus\\{\\widetilde r\\})\n\\]\nreads the coefficient of \\(I\\) in a simplicial top cycle.</p><p>By regularity of \\(r\\), Lemma 4.1, and (4.9), this coefficient is one. The same holds for every facet.</p><p>Since a \\((k-1)\\)-dimensional complex has no simplicial \\(k\\)-chains, its top homology classes are exactly its top cycles. Thus \\(f_*[\\mathbb P(V)]\\) is represented by the sum of all facets. Its boundary vanishes. Each codimension-one face consequently occurs in an even number of facets, and purity makes that number positive.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.2, local counting", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The fiber \\(q^{-1}(y)\\) is finite, and the image of the total fundamental class of \\(M\\) in \\( H_n(Y,Y\\setminus\\{y\\})\\cong\\mathbb F_2 \\) is \\(|q^{-1}(y)|\\) modulo two.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This is a general local-counting lemma; no admissible map is assumed.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(M\\) be a closed smooth \\(n\\)-manifold, possibly disconnected, and let \\(q:M\\to Y\\) be continuous, with \\(Y\\) Hausdorff.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(y\\in Y\\) has a neighborhood homeomorphic to an open subset of \\(\\mathbb R^n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(q\\) is smooth in these coordinates near \\(q^{-1}(y)\\), with invertible derivative at every point of that fiber.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The fiber \\(q^{-1}(y)\\) is finite, and the image of the total fundamental class of \\(M\\) in\n\\[\nH_n(Y,Y\\setminus\\{y\\})\\cong\\mathbb F_2\n\\]\nis \\(|q^{-1}(y)|\\) modulo two.</p><p>In particular, if \\(Y\\) is a connected closed \\(n\\)-manifold, this parity is the mod-two degree of \\(q\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The inverse function theorem makes every point of the fiber isolated. The fiber is closed in compact \\(M\\), hence finite. By excision,\n\\[\nH_n(M,M\\setminus q^{-1}(y))\n\\cong\n\\bigoplus_{x\\in q^{-1}(y)}\nH_n(M,M\\setminus\\{x\\}).\n\\]\nThe fundamental class has coordinate one in each summand. A local diffeomorphism sends each local generator to the generator at \\(y\\). Naturality of relative homology now gives the assertion.</p><p>This argument uses no smoothness away from the selected fiber.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "One has \\( a\\ge h, \\qquad s\\le k. \\) On \\(U_I\\), \\(S(x)=I\\), and \\( U_I\\subseteq\\mathbb P(V)\\setminus\\mathbb P(x_0^\\perp). \\) Choose a regular value \\(r\\in\\mathring\\Delta_I\\) of \\(f_I\\), and extend it by zero on \\(J\\) …", "statement": "<p>Facet level sets, sphere bundles, and odd local counts; the first part of the proof of paper Theorem 4.3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the admissible map \\(f:\\mathbb P(V)\\to\\Delta^{m-1}\\) from Proposition 4, with \\(\\dim V=k\\ge3\\) and \\(m=N_k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a facet \\(I\\) of \\(K\\), and set\n  \\[\n  s=|I|,\n  \\qquad\n  J=[m]\\setminus I,\n  \\qquad\n  a=|J|,\n  \\qquad\n  h=N_{k-1}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a line \\(x_0\\) with \\(S(x_0)=I\\), and define\n  \\[\n  U_I=\\{x\\in\\mathbb P(V):f_i(x)&gt;0\\text{ for every }i\\in I\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathring\\Delta_I\\) be the open simplex on \\(I\\), and let \\(f_I\\) denote the \\(I\\)-coordinate map on \\(U_I\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(x\\in U_I\\), apply Proposition 2 to the restriction of \\(f\\) to \\(\\mathbb P(x^\\perp)\\), retaining the coordinates in \\(J\\), and denote its odd sphere map by\n  \\[\n  G_x:\\Sigma(x^\\perp)\\longrightarrow\\Sigma_a.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One has\n\\[\na\\ge h,\n\\qquad\ns\\le k. \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nOn \\(U_I\\), \\(S(x)=I\\), and\n\\[\nU_I\\subseteq\\mathbb P(V)\\setminus\\mathbb P(x_0^\\perp).\n\\]</p><p>Choose a regular value \\(r\\in\\mathring\\Delta_I\\) of \\(f_I\\), and extend it by zero on \\(J\\) to obtain \\(\\widetilde r\\). Then\n\\[\nL=f_I^{-1}(r)=f^{-1}(\\widetilde r)\n\\]\nis a compact smooth manifold without boundary, of dimension\n\\[\n\\dim L=k-s=a-h. \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\nInitially \\(L\\) may be allowed to be empty.</p><p>The sphere bundle\n\\[\nM=\n\\{(x,B):x\\in L,\\ B\\in\\operatorname{Sym}(x^\\perp),\\\n\\operatorname{tr}|B|=1\\}\n\\]\nhas an antipodally compatible trivialization\n\\[\nM\\cong L\\times S^{h-1},\n\\qquad\n(x,B)\\mapsto(x,-B)\n\\ \\longleftrightarrow\\\n(x,z)\\mapsto(x,-z), \\tag{4.3}\n\\]\nand \\(\\dim M=a-1\\).</p><p>The map \\(G(x,B)=G_x(B)\\) is continuous and smooth over the open negative orthant of \\(\\Sigma_a\\). One can choose a regular value \\(g\\) in that orthant. For a fixed \\(0&lt;\\lambda&lt;1\\), let\n\\[\ny=(\\lambda r,(1-\\lambda)g)\\in\\Sigma_m,\n\\]\nwith coordinate blocks indexed by \\(I\\) and \\(J\\). Then\n\\[\nG^{-1}(g)\\longrightarrow F^{-1}(y),\n\\qquad\n(x,B)\\longmapsto\\lambda P_x+(1-\\lambda)B,\n\\]\nis a bijection, \\(y\\) is regular in the local sense of Lemma 4.1, and\n\\[\n|G^{-1}(g)|=|F^{-1}(y)|\\equiv1\\pmod2. \\tag{4.7}\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(L\\ne\\varnothing\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Every line in \\(x_0^\\perp\\) uses only labels in \\(J\\). Proposition 3, applied in \\(x_0^\\perp\\), gives\n\\[\na\\ge h,\n\\qquad\ns=m-a\\le N_k-N_{k-1}=k.\n\\]</p><p>Maximality of \\(I\\) implies \\(S(x)=I\\) for every \\(x\\in U_I\\). Thus \\(f_I\\) takes \\(U_I\\) smoothly to \\(\\mathring\\Delta_I\\). Moreover, \\(U_I\\) lies in\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(V)\\setminus\\mathbb P(x_0^\\perp),\n\\]\nbecause a line orthogonal to \\(x_0\\) cannot use any label in \\(I\\).</p><p>Choose a regular value \\(r\\in\\mathring\\Delta_I\\) using Sard’s theorem. Positivity of the coordinates of \\(r\\) puts every preimage of \\(\\widetilde r\\) in \\(U_I\\), so\n\\[\nL=f_I^{-1}(r)=f^{-1}(\\widetilde r).\n\\]\nHence \\(L\\) is compact. The regular-level-set theorem gives a smooth manifold without boundary of dimension\n\\[\n(k-1)-(s-1)=k-s=a-h.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(x\\in U_I\\), admissibility ensures that \\(f\\) on \\(\\mathbb P(x^\\perp)\\) uses only labels in \\(J\\). The subspace-restriction and continuity properties in Proposition 2 make the maps \\(G_x\\) a continuous map on the corresponding sphere bundle.</p><p>The bundle \\(x\\mapsto x^\\perp\\) is trivial on the affine chart above. Choose the unit representative \\(u\\) of \\(x\\) having positive inner product with a fixed unit representative of \\(x_0\\). Orthogonal projection from \\(x_0^\\perp\\) onto \\(u^\\perp\\) is invertible and varies smoothly with \\(x\\); orthonormalizing its images of a fixed basis gives a smooth orthonormal trivialization.</p><p>The induced trivialization of symmetric operators, followed by radial change from trace norm to a Euclidean norm, gives (4.3). Equip \\(M\\) with the smooth structure given by the Euclidean sphere bundle. The trace-norm parametrization is smooth near every definite \\(B\\), which is all that will be needed. In particular,\n\\[\n\\dim M=(a-h)+(h-1)=a-1. \\tag{4.4}\n\\]</p><p>Consider the open negative orthant of \\(\\Sigma_a\\). At a preimage of this orthant, \\(B\\) has no positive spectral subspace. It has no kernel either: the exact support property says that all \\(J\\) labels occur on its range, leaving no label for a kernel line. Thus \\(B\\) is negative definite on \\(x^\\perp\\). Consequently \\(G\\) is smooth on the preimage of the negative orthant.</p><p>Sard’s theorem allows a regular value \\(g\\) in that orthant. Its preimage is finite by compactness, including the possibility of an empty preimage.</p><p>Fix \\(0&lt;\\lambda&lt;1\\) and form\n\\[\ny=(\\lambda r,(1-\\lambda)g).\n\\]\nEvery \\(A\\in F^{-1}(y)\\) is nonsingular by the full-support property. All lines in its nonzero positive spectral subspace use only labels in \\(I\\). That subspace must be one-dimensional: a subspace of dimension at least two intersects \\(x_0^\\perp\\), where no label in \\(I\\) is available.</p><p>Call the resulting positive line \\(x\\). The positive output block has every \\(I\\) coordinate nonzero, so \\(S(x)=I\\) and \\(x\\in U_I\\). Since the sum of the positive coordinates of \\(F(A)\\) is \\(\\lambda\\), the positive eigenvalue is \\(\\lambda\\). Therefore\n\\[\nA=\\lambda P_x+(1-\\lambda)B,\n\\qquad\nB&lt;0\\text{ on }x^\\perp,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{tr}|B|=1. \\tag{4.5}\n\\]\nThe subspace formula gives \\(f_I(x)=r\\) and \\(G_x(B)=g\\). Conversely, every \\((x,B)\\in G^{-1}(g)\\) gives a preimage by (4.5).</p><p>To verify local regularity of \\(y\\), near a preimage use \\((\\lambda',x',B')\\) as coordinates for symmetric operators of signature \\((1,k-1)\\), with \\(B'\\) negative definite and normalized on \\(x'^\\perp\\). The single positive eigenvalue and its line vary smoothly, so these are smooth coordinates on the trace-norm sphere near that preimage.</p><p>In the target orthant, use the positive mass followed by the two normalized coordinate blocks. In these coordinates,\n\\[\nF:(\\lambda',x',B')\n\\longmapsto\n(\\lambda',f_I(x'),G_{x'}(B')). \\tag{4.6}\n\\]\nThe first derivative block is the identity. The second is surjective because \\(r\\) is regular for \\(f_I\\). On the kernel of these two blocks, the third block is the derivative of \\(G|_M\\), which is surjective because \\(g\\) is regular.</p><p>Thus the full derivative is surjective, and is an isomorphism since source and target both have dimension \\(m-1\\). By Proposition 3 and Lemma 4.1,\n\\[\n|G^{-1}(g)|=|F^{-1}(y)|\\equiv1\\pmod2.\n\\]\nThis also proves that \\(L\\) is nonempty.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every facet has \\(s=k\\) vertices.", "statement": "<p>Facet size and facet coefficients; the remaining local-counting part of paper Theorem 4.3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the setting of Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">For a facet \\(I\\), put\n  \\[\n  s=|I|,\n  \\quad\n  J=[m]\\setminus I,\n  \\quad\n  a=|J|,\n  \\quad\n  h=N_{k-1}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use a regular value \\(r\\in\\mathring\\Delta_I\\), its zero extension \\(\\widetilde r\\), the compact level set \\(L=f^{-1}(\\widetilde r)\\), and a regular negative value \\(g\\) of the sphere-bundle map \\(G\\), chosen as in Lemma 4.2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every facet has \\(s=k\\) vertices. For each facet, the chosen regular level satisfies\n\\[\n|f^{-1}(\\widetilde r)|=|L|\\equiv1\\pmod2. \\tag{4.9}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The oddness of \\(G\\) in the sphere variable gives a quotient map\n\\[\n\\overline G:\nL\\times\\mathbb RP^{h-1}\n\\longrightarrow\n\\mathbb RP^{a-1}.\n\\]\nWrite\n\\[\nw\\in H^1(\\mathbb RP^{a-1}),\n\\qquad\nu\\in H^1(\\mathbb RP^{h-1})\n\\]\nfor the first Stiefel–Whitney classes of the real line bundles associated with their antipodal double covers.</p><p>Equivariance identifies the pullback of the target sphere cover with\n\\[\nL\\times S^{h-1}\n\\longrightarrow\nL\\times\\mathbb RP^{h-1}.\n\\]\nIndeed, the identification sends\n\\[\n(x,z)\\longmapsto((x,[z]),G(x,z))\n\\]\nand is an isomorphism on each two-point fiber. This cover isomorphism induces an isomorphism of the associated real line bundles. Naturality of first Stiefel–Whitney classes gives\n\\[\n\\overline G^{\\,*}w=\\operatorname{pr}_2^*u. \\tag{4.8}\n\\]\nThere is no contribution from \\(H^1(L)\\) in this identity.</p><p>Suppose \\(a&gt;h\\). The projective-space cohomology calculation gives\n\\[\nu^h=0,\n\\qquad\n\\overline G^{\\,*}(w^{a-1})\n=\n\\operatorname{pr}_2^*(u^{a-1})\n=\n0.\n\\]\nBoth source and target of \\(\\overline G\\) are closed manifolds of dimension \\(a-1\\), and \\(w^{a-1}\\) is the target’s top cohomology generator. Evaluation on the total fundamental class therefore gives mod-two degree zero.</p><p>On the other hand, \\([g]\\) is a regular value in local quotient coordinates, and every preimage of \\([g]\\) has exactly one representative mapping to \\(g\\). Its preimage count is therefore the odd number in (4.7), contradicting Lemma 4.1. Together with \\(a\\ge h\\), this proves \\(a=h\\) and \\(s=k\\) for every facet.</p><p>Now \\(\\dim L=0\\), so \\(L\\) is a finite set. For each \\(x\\in L\\), the map\n\\[\nG_x:\\Sigma(x^\\perp)\\longrightarrow\\Sigma_a\n\\]\nis odd between spheres of the same dimension \\(h-1\\). Regularity of \\(g\\) for \\(G\\) implies its regularity for each such fiber map. Each fiber therefore contributes an odd number to (4.7), and hence\n\\[\n|f^{-1}(\\widetilde r)|=|L|\\equiv1\\pmod2.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The triangles of the support complex of a smooth admissible map \\( f:\\mathbb RP^2\\longrightarrow\\Delta^5 \\) form a six-label triangle system.", "statement": "<p>Six-label triangle systems and their rigidity.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Following Definition 5.1 of the paper, a six-label triangle system on a six-element set \\(C\\) is a collection\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal T\\subseteq\\binom C3\n  \\]\n  such that:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. Exactly one of \\(T\\) and \\(C\\setminus T\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal T\\) for every \\(T\\in\\binom C3\\).</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. Every pair of elements of \\(C\\) belongs to exactly two members of \\(\\mathcal T\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(a\\in C\\), its link graph has vertex set \\(C\\setminus\\{a\\}\\), and edge \\(\\{u,v\\}\\) precisely when \\(\\{a,u,v\\}\\in\\mathcal T\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In a support complex, a triangle is a face with three vertices and a tetrahedron is a face with four vertices. These names do not assert that the faces are geometric simplices in projective space.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The triangles of the support complex of a smooth admissible map\n\\[\nf:\\mathbb RP^2\\longrightarrow\\Delta^5\n\\]\nform a six-label triangle system.</p><p>Every six-label triangle system has the following properties:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Every vertex link is a cycle of length five.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Every four-element subset contains a triangle, but does not contain all four of its triples as triangles.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. If \\(a,b\\in C\\) are distinct and \\(R\\subseteq C\\setminus\\{a,b\\}\\) has three elements, the link graphs at \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), restricted to \\(R\\), are different. In particular, no transposition of labels preserves the system.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. The triangles contained in any five-element subset of \\(C\\) determine the system uniquely.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 5.1 derives a six-label triangle system from an admissible map on \\(\\mathbb RP^2\\). Lemma 5.2 proves the four stated properties for every such system.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The triangles of \\(K\\) form a six-label triangle system.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(f:\\mathbb RP^2\\to\\Delta^5\\) be a smooth admissible map.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K\\) be its support complex on the six labels \\([6]\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The triangles of \\(K\\) form a six-label triangle system.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Proposition 4, the complex is pure of dimension two, has all fifteen edges, and each edge belongs to a positive even number of triangles.</p><p>Two complementary triples cannot both be triangles: choose witness lines \\(x,y\\subset\\mathbb R^3\\) whose supports contain the respective triples. A line perpendicular to both exists and, by admissibility, could use none of the six labels.</p><p>There are ten complementary pairs of triples, so there are at most ten triangles. Counting incidences between triangles and edges gives at least\n\\[\n\\frac{2\\cdot15}{3}=10\n\\]\ntriangles. Equality holds throughout: there is one triangle in each complementary pair, and every edge occurs twice.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Lemma 5.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every vertex link is a cycle of length five.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For this lemma no admissible map is assumed.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C\\) be a six-element set and \\(\\mathcal T\\) an arbitrary six-label triangle system, as defined in Proposition 5.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. Every vertex link is a cycle of length five.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Every four-element subset of \\(C\\) contains a triangle, but does not contain all four of its triples as triangles.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. If \\(a,b\\in C\\) are distinct and \\(R\\subseteq C\\setminus\\{a,b\\}\\) has cardinality three, the link graphs at \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), restricted to \\(R\\), are different. In particular, no transposition of two labels preserves \\(\\mathcal T\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">4. The triangles contained in any five-element subset of \\(C\\) determine \\(\\mathcal T\\) uniquely.</p>", "proof": "<p>Every vertex of the link at \\(a\\) has degree two, since the corresponding pair with \\(a\\) lies in exactly two triangles. A simple graph in which every vertex has degree two is a disjoint union of cycles of length at least three. On five vertices it must therefore be a single five-cycle.</p><p>Let \\(A\\subseteq C\\) have four elements and choose \\(a\\in A\\). A five-cycle has no independent set of size three: if three vertices were independent, the three gaps following them around the cycle would each contain another vertex, requiring at least six vertices. Thus the other three elements of \\(A\\) contain an edge of the link at \\(a\\), giving a triangle in \\(A\\).</p><p>If all four triples in \\(A\\) were triangles, the link at \\(a\\) would contain the three-cycle on \\(A\\setminus\\{a\\}\\), which a five-cycle cannot contain.</p><p>For the third assertion, write\n\\[\nC=\\{a,b,c,d,e,f\\},\n\\]\nwhere the two triangles containing \\(\\{a,b\\}\\) are \\(\\{a,b,c\\}\\) and \\(\\{a,b,d\\}\\). Deleting \\(b\\) from the five-cycle at \\(a\\) leaves a path on \\(\\{c,d,e,f\\}\\) with endpoints \\(c,d\\). Its middle edge is \\(\\{e,f\\}\\), so its other two edges are one of the matchings\n\\[\nM_1=\\{\\{c,e\\},\\{d,f\\}\\},\n\\qquad\nM_2=\\{\\{c,f\\},\\{d,e\\}\\}.\n\\]</p><p>Deleting \\(a\\) from the link at \\(b\\) gives a path with the same endpoints and middle edge. The two paths must use opposite matchings. For example, if the first uses \\(M_1\\), the triangles \\(\\{a,c,e\\}\\) and \\(\\{a,d,f\\}\\) exclude their complementary triples \\(\\{b,d,f\\}\\) and \\(\\{b,c,e\\}\\), forcing the second path to use \\(M_2\\).</p><p>The symmetric difference of the two paths is the four-cycle with edges \\(M_1\\cup M_2\\). Every three of its four vertices retain an edge, so the restrictions of the paths to any such triple differ. A transposition \\((a\\ b)\\) preserving the triangle system would make the two restricted link graphs identical, which is impossible.</p><p>Finally, if \\(z\\) is the omitted vertex of a five-element subset, every triple containing \\(z\\) has its complementary triple in that subset. The complementary-triple rule therefore recovers its membership in \\(\\mathcal T\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The complex \\(K\\) is pure of dimension three.", "statement": "<p>Complementary triangle systems and local incidence on ten labels.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Suppose\n  \\[\n  f:\\mathbb RP^3\\longrightarrow\\Delta^9\n  \\]\n  is a smooth admissible map, and let \\(K\\) be its support complex on \\([10]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a tetrahedron \\(S\\) of \\(K\\), write \\(C=[10]\\setminus S\\), and let \\(\\mathcal T_C\\) be the collection of triangles of \\(K\\) contained in \\(C\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For an edge \\(D\\) of \\(K\\), its link graph has vertices the labels \\(v\\notin D\\) for which \\(D\\cup\\{v\\}\\) is a triangle, and edges the pairs \\(\\{v,w\\}\\) for which \\(D\\cup\\{v,w\\}\\) is a tetrahedron.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The complex \\(K\\) is pure of dimension three. For every tetrahedron \\(S\\), the collection \\(\\mathcal T_{[10]\\setminus S}\\) is a six-label triangle system. No two tetrahedra of \\(K\\) are disjoint.</p><p>Every triangle belongs to exactly two tetrahedra. If two tetrahedra differ by exchanging labels \\(a,b\\), the same exchange carries their complementary triangle systems to one another.</p><p>Every connected component of an edge link is a cycle of length three or four.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 4 gives purity and positive even tetrahedral multiplicity at each triangle. Lemma 6.1 identifies every complementary six-label triangle system and rules out disjoint tetrahedra. Lemma 6.2 gives transport across adjacent tetrahedra and exact multiplicity two. Lemma 6.3 gives the asserted short cycles in edge links.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The triangles of \\(K\\) contained in \\(C\\) form a six-label triangle system.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the admissible map \\(f:\\mathbb RP^3\\to\\Delta^9\\) and support complex \\(K\\) from Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\) be a tetrahedron of \\(K\\), and put \\(C=[10]\\setminus S\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The triangles of \\(K\\) contained in \\(C\\) form a six-label triangle system. In particular, two tetrahedra of \\(K\\) cannot be disjoint.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 4 says that \\(K\\) is pure of dimension three. A witness line \\(x\\) for the facet \\(S\\) has support exactly \\(S\\). The restriction of \\(f\\) to\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(x^\\perp)\\cong\\mathbb RP^2\n\\]\nuses only labels in \\(C\\).</p><p>It uses all six: otherwise the lower bound in Proposition 3, applied in dimension three, would be violated. By Proposition 5, this restriction supplies a six-label triangle system contained among the triangles of \\(K\\) on \\(C\\).</p><p>There are no additional triangles on \\(C\\). Otherwise some complementary triples \\(T,C\\setminus T\\) would both be triangles of \\(K\\). Take witness lines for \\(S,T,C\\setminus T\\). In \\(\\mathbb R^4\\) there is a line perpendicular to all three; their supports together contain all ten labels, leaving that line no admissible label.</p><p>If a tetrahedron \\(S'\\) were disjoint from \\(S\\), its four triples would all be triangles on \\(C\\). This contradicts Proposition 5(2).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.2, including its initial transport observation", "depth": 1, "snippet": "If two tetrahedra differ by exchanging labels \\(a,b\\), the transposition \\((a\\ b)\\) carries their complementary triangle systems to one another.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(f\\), \\(K\\), and the complementary triangle systems \\(\\mathcal T_C\\) from Proposition 6.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If two tetrahedra differ by exchanging labels \\(a,b\\), the transposition \\((a\\ b)\\) carries their complementary triangle systems to one another.</p><p>Every triangle of \\(K\\) belongs to exactly two tetrahedra.</p>", "proof": "<p>If two tetrahedra differ by exchanging \\(a,b\\), their complements share five labels, on which both systems are the triangles of \\(K\\). After the exchange these triangles remain fixed, so the systems agree everywhere by Proposition 5(4).</p><p>Suppose three tetrahedra\n\\[\nT\\cup\\{a\\},\n\\qquad\nT\\cup\\{b\\},\n\\qquad\nT\\cup\\{c\\}\n\\]\ncontain the same triangle \\(T\\). Put\n\\[\nR=[10]\\setminus(T\\cup\\{a,b,c\\}),\n\\]\nwhich has four elements. Transporting the complementary systems successively by\n\\[\nR\\cup\\{b,c\\}\n\\xrightarrow{(a\\ b)}\nR\\cup\\{a,c\\}\n\\xrightarrow{(b\\ c)}\nR\\cup\\{a,b\\}\n\\xrightarrow{(c\\ a)}\nR\\cup\\{b,c\\}\n\\]\npreserves the initial system.</p><p>The composite fixes \\(R\\) and exchanges \\(b,c\\), contradicting Proposition 5(3). Thus at most two tetrahedra contain any triangle. Proposition 4 gives positive even multiplicity, so every triangle belongs to exactly two tetrahedra.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "Lemma 6.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every connected component of an edge link is a cycle of length three or four.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(K\\) and the edge-link graphs defined in Proposition 6.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every connected component of an edge link is a cycle of length three or four.</p>", "proof": "<p>Every vertex of an edge link has degree two by Lemma 6.2, so each component is a simple cycle. We show that the link contains no path on five distinct vertices.</p><p>Suppose such a path has consecutive vertices \\(v_0,v_1,v_2,v_3,v_4\\), and let \\(D\\) be the edge whose link contains it. Let \\(R\\) be the three labels outside\n\\[\nD\\cup\\{v_0,v_1,v_2,v_3,v_4\\}.\n\\]\nThe four successive edges give tetrahedra \\(D\\cup\\{v_i,v_{i+1}\\}\\), whose complementary systems are transported, by Lemma 6.2, as follows:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nR\\cup\\{v_2,v_3,v_4\\}\n&amp;\\xrightarrow{(v_0\\ v_2)}\nR\\cup\\{v_0,v_3,v_4\\}\\\\\n&amp;\\xrightarrow{(v_1\\ v_3)}\nR\\cup\\{v_0,v_1,v_4\\}\\\\\n&amp;\\xrightarrow{(v_2\\ v_4)}\nR\\cup\\{v_0,v_1,v_2\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p><p>The composite fixes \\(R\\) and sends \\(v_4\\) to \\(v_2\\). Hence, for every pair \\(\\{r,s\\}\\subseteq R\\), the first system contains \\(\\{v_4,r,s\\}\\) if and only if the last contains \\(\\{v_2,r,s\\}\\).</p><p>The latter triple belongs to both complementary sets, so its membership in either system is simply its membership in \\(K\\). The first system therefore has identical link graphs at \\(v_4\\) and \\(v_2\\) when restricted to \\(R\\), contradicting Proposition 5(3).</p><p>Every cycle of length at least five contains such a path, proving the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Lemma 6.4, the block structure", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The sets \\(B_y\\) are disjoint and have at least two elements.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(f:\\mathbb RP^3\\to\\Delta^9\\) is a smooth admissible map, with support complex \\(K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a tetrahedron \\(S\\) of \\(K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(v\\in S\\), let \\(p(v)\\) be the unique label outside \\(S\\) such that\n  \\[\n  (S\\setminus\\{v\\})\\cup\\{p(v)\\}\n  \\]\n  is a tetrahedron.</p><p class=\"li\">For every distinct value \\(y\\in p(S)\\), set\n  \\[\n  B_y=p^{-1}(y)\\cup\\{y\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The sets \\(B_y\\) are disjoint and have at least two elements. For every choice \\(q_y\\in B_y\\), the set\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{y\\in p(S)}(B_y\\setminus\\{q_y\\})\n\\]\nis a tetrahedron.</p><p>Moreover, every tetrahedron of \\(K\\) contains at least two elements of one of these blocks.</p>", "proof": "<p>The partner \\(p(v)\\) exists and is unique by Proposition 6, applied to the triangle \\(S\\setminus\\{v\\}\\). It lies outside \\(S\\), since the only tetrahedron on the vertices of \\(S\\) is \\(S\\) itself. The stated disjointness and lower size bound are immediate.</p><p>First prove that replacing \\(v\\) by \\(a=p(v)\\) leaves the collection of blocks unchanged. Put\n\\[\nS'=(S\\setminus\\{v\\})\\cup\\{a\\},\n\\]\nand let \\(p'\\) be its partner map. One has \\(p'(a)=v\\).</p><p>For \\(w\\in S\\setminus\\{v\\}\\), put\n\\[\nD=S\\setminus\\{v,w\\},\n\\qquad\nb=p(w).\n\\]\nThe link of \\(D\\) contains the edges\n\\[\n\\{v,w\\},\n\\qquad\n\\{w,a\\},\n\\qquad\n\\{v,b\\}.\n\\]</p><p>If \\(a=b\\), these form a three-cycle. Since every link vertex has degree two, that cycle is a whole component, and the other tetrahedron on \\(D\\cup\\{a\\}\\) is \\(D\\cup\\{a,v\\}\\). Thus \\(p'(w)=v\\).</p><p>If \\(a\\ne b\\), the displayed edges form the path \\(a,w,v,b\\) on four distinct vertices. Proposition 6 forces it to complete to a four-cycle, with edge \\(\\{a,b\\}\\). Thus \\(p'(w)=b=p(w)\\).</p><p>Therefore\n\\[\np'(a)=v,\n\\qquad\np'(w)=\n\\begin{cases}\nv,&amp;p(w)=a,\\\\\np(w),&amp;p(w)\\ne a.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThe block formerly indexed by \\(a\\) is now indexed by \\(v\\) and has the same elements; all other blocks are unchanged.</p><p>The tetrahedron \\(S\\) contains all elements of each block except its indexing partner. The same is true after any such exchange, by block invariance. To change the omitted element of a block from \\(q\\) to \\(u\\), replace the present element \\(u\\) by its partner \\(q\\). Doing this once in each block where a change is wanted realizes every possible choice of omissions.</p><p>These sets have cardinality four, since\n\\[\n\\sum_{y\\in p(S)}(|B_y|-1)=|S|=4.\n\\]</p><p>Finally, suppose a tetrahedron \\(T\\) met each block in at most one element. In each block omit that element if present, and otherwise omit any element. The resulting tetrahedron lies in the union of the blocks and is disjoint from \\(T\\), contrary to Proposition 6.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Theorem 6.5, the obstruction on ten labels", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is no admissible map \\( \\mathbb RP^3\\longrightarrow\\Delta^9. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Admissibility means the smooth nonnegative coordinate condition, sum-one condition, and orthogonal-support disjointness defined in Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is no admissible map\n\\[\n\\mathbb RP^3\\longrightarrow\\Delta^9.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Assume such a map exists and fix a tetrahedron \\(S\\) of its support complex \\(K\\). Let \\(t\\) be the number of blocks in Proposition 7, and let \\(R\\) be the set of labels outside their union. The blocks contain \\(4+t\\) labels in total, so\n\\[\n1\\le t\\le4,\n\\qquad\n|R|=6-t.\n\\]</p><p>If \\(t=4\\), each block has size two and \\(S\\) meets each in just one element, contradicting the last assertion of Proposition 7. Thus \\(t\\le3\\).</p><p>There is no triangle entirely in \\(R\\). By purity, such a triangle would extend to a tetrahedron; its one remaining vertex could not supply two vertices in a block.</p><p>On the other hand, \\(R\\subseteq[10]\\setminus S\\), where every four labels contain a triangle by Propositions 6 and 5(2). Consequently \\(|R|\\le3\\), and therefore \\(t\\ge3\\).</p><p>It follows that\n\\[\nt=3,\n\\qquad\n|R|=3,\n\\]\nand the block sizes are \\(3,2,2\\).</p><p>The complement of \\(S\\) consists of \\(R\\) and one omitted element from each block. Choose distinct labels \\(r,s\\in R\\). In the triangle system on this complement, \\(\\{r,s\\}\\) belongs to two distinct triangles. Their third vertices cannot be in \\(R\\), because \\(R\\) contains no triangle. They are therefore two distinct omitted block elements.</p><p>Only one block has size three, so at least one of these triangles is \\(\\{r,s,z\\}\\) with \\(z\\) in a block \\(B\\) of size two.</p><p>Every tetrahedron containing \\(\\{r,s,z\\}\\) must contain two vertices of some block. As \\(r,s\\) lie outside all blocks, its fourth vertex must be the other element of \\(B\\). Thus this triangle belongs to at most one tetrahedron, contradicting the exact multiplicity two in Proposition 6.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [2, 0, "cite"], [2, 21, "cite"], [3, 2, "cite"], [3, 4, "cite"], [4, 2, "cite"], [5, 8, "cite"], [5, 9, "cite"], [5, 11, "cite"], [6, 5, "cite"], [6, 7, "cite"], [7, 5, "cite"], [8, 9, "cite"], [8, 11, "cite"], [8, 17, "cite"], [9, 14, "cite"], [9, 16, "cite"], [9, 17, "cite"], [9, 18, "cite"], [10, 9, "cite"], [10, 11, "cite"], [10, 12, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [12, 9, "cite"], [13, 17, "cite"], [13, 18, "cite"], [13, 19, "cite"], [13, 21, "cite"], [14, 13, "cite"], [15, 13, "cite"], [16, 20, "cite"], [16, 21, "cite"], [17, 16, "cite"], [18, 16, "cite"], [18, 19, "cite"], [19, 16, "cite"], [20, 21, "cite"], [21, 0, "cite"]]});
